    <DataNode elementId="AT2G24190-MONOMER" textLabel="aldehyde reductase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9ZUH5" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">CytADR is an aldehyde reductase (ADR) that catalyzes the reduction of the carbonyl groups on saturated and alpha,beta-unsaturated aldehydes with 5 or more carbons &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;. It is a member of the short-chain dehydrogenase/reductase (SDR) family of proteins |CITS: [21169366]|. It is believed that this enzyme localizes to the cytosol like the closely-related |FRAME: AT3G61220 ADR| protein where they likely help to detoxify reactive carbonyls |CITS: [21169366]|. Interestingly, &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; this protein is also able to reduce |FRAME: METHYL-GLYOXAL|, an &amp;alpha;-dicarbonyl compound that may be produced during glycolysis, fatty acid metabolism, and protein metabolism |CITS: [21169366]|. Two chloroplastic (|FRAME: AT1G54870| and |FRAME: AT3G04000|) as well as a second cytosolic |FRAME: AT3G61220 ADR| in |FRAME: ORG-5993| with similar enzymatic activity have also been characterized |CITS: [21169366]|.</Comment>
